WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.000
When Donald Trump won the presidential election&nbsp;in 2016,

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.301
even though 2.8 million more people voted for Hillary Clinton,

00:00:08.301 --> 00:00:13.078
everyone on the left from Bill de Blasio to Michael Moore to Eric Holder to Bill Maher

00:00:13.078 --> 00:00:17.840
said that at long last, "we have to just abolish the Electoral College."

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.282
Then California Senator Barbara Boxer introduced&nbsp;a bill

00:00:21.282 --> 00:00:24.468
to amend the U.S. Constitution to do just that.

00:00:24.468 --> 00:00:26.830
A Gallup poll from September of this year

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:31.525
showed that 61 percent of Americans support abolishing the Electoral College

00:00:31.525 --> 00:00:33.734
in favor of&nbsp;a national popular vote.

00:00:33.734 --> 00:00:36.582
Although it's an issue that breaks along partisan lines

00:00:36.582 --> 00:00:40.719
with 77 percent of Republicans wanting to keep the Electoral College

00:00:40.719 --> 00:00:44.248
and 89 percent of Democrats saying we&nbsp;should get rid of it.

00:00:44.248 --> 00:00:47.668
Is the Electoral College the best system for electing a president?

00:00:47.668 --> 00:00:51.110
That was the subject of an online Soho Forum debate

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:53.901
held on Wednesday, November 11, 2020.

00:00:53.901 --> 00:00:56.909
Richard&nbsp;Epstein, a law professor at New York University,

00:00:56.909 --> 00:01:00.821
defended the system against Lawrence Lessig, a law professor at Harvard.

00:01:00.821 --> 00:01:03.238
Here's Richard Epstein and Lawrence Lessig

00:01:03.238 --> 00:01:07.363
in an online debate moderated by Soho Forum Director Gene Epstein.

